"University of Glasgow - Schools - School of Mathematics & Statistics - Events - Event details (https://www.gla.ac.uk/schools/mathematicsstatistics/events/details/?id=11755)\nciteturn4search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; If time permits, I will also discuss how one may compute the number of projective crepant resolutions admitted by a quiver variety, and ongoing work in this direction. This is all based on joint work (arXiv:2510.15160) with Pavel Shlykov.\n\n# Nakajima quiver varieties in dimension four\n\n### Samuel Lewis (University of Oxford)\n\nWednesday 28th October 16:00-17:00\nMaths 311B\n\n#### Abstract\n\nNakajima quiver varieties form an important class of examples of conical symplectic singularities. Notable examples of these include Hilbert schemes of points on surfaces, nilpotent orbit closures, and Kleinian singularities. In this talk I will describe a classification, obtained combinatorially, of the four-dimensional quiver varieties. The theory of root systems is then applied to describe the geometric structure of these varieties, obtaining a classification of their symplectic leaves, the minimal degenerations between them, and their Namikawa Weyl group. If time permits, I will also discuss how one may compute the number of projective crepant resolutions admitted by a quiver variety, and ongoing work in this direction. This is all based on joint work (arXiv:2510.15160) with Pavel Shlykov.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSamuel Lewis | School of Mathematics Research (https://www.bristolmathsresearch.org/seminar/samuel-lewis/)\nciteturn4search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 months ago; If time permits, I will also discuss how one may compute the number of projective crepant resolutions admitted by the quiver varieties, and some conjectures on the resulting numerology. This is all based on joint work with Pavel Shlykov (arXiv:2510.15160).\n\n# Samuel Lewis\n\nUniversity of Oxford University of Oxford\n\n### Quiver varieties in dimension four\n\nAlgebra Seminar\n\n### \n\n17th February 2026, 4:00 pm – 5:00 pm\nFry Building, 2.04\n\nNakajima quiver varieties form an important class of examples of conical symplectic singularities, such as the well-studied Kleinian singularities in dimension two. In this talk I will describe a classification of the four-dimensional quiver varieties, obtained combinatorially and comprising dimension vectors in three “Types”. The theory of root systems is then applied to describe the geometric structure of these varieties in detail, such as symplectic leaves, their minimal degenerations, and the Namikawa Weyl group. If time permits, I will also discuss how one may compute the number of projective crepant resolutions admitted by the quiver varieties, and some conjectures on the resulting numerology. This is all based on joint work with Pavel Shlykov (arXiv:2510.15160).\n\nOrganisers: Eoghan McDowell, Vlad Vankov--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCombinatorics and asymptotic behavior for double Hurwitz numbers | alphaXiv (https://www.alphaxiv.org/abs/2604.26323)\nciteturn4search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; Crawled: last month; @misc{li2026combinatoricsasymptoticbehaviordouble, title={Combinatorics and asymptotic behavior for double Hurwitz numbers}, author={Xiang Li}, year={2026}, eprint={2604.26323}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math-ph}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.26323}, }\nSubmitted 29 Apr 2026\n\n# Combinatorics and asymptotic behavior for double Hurwitz numbers\n\nXL\n\nXiang Li\n\n## Abstract\n\nPolynomial-in-time algorithms for computing classical Hurwitz numbers were given in [4] based on the Pandharipande equation. The paritition function of double Hurwitz numbers was proved [21] to satisfy the 2-Toda hierarchy. In this paper, similar to [21] we derive Pandharipande-type equations for double Hurwitz numbers from 2-Toda hierarchy. Based on these equations and a method from [4], we study large genus as well as large degree asymptotics of double Hurwitz numbers.\n\nView Paper\n\n## AI Overview\n\nNo overview yet. Generate one to research the paper and explain its contribution.\n\n## Audio\n\nGenerate audio summary→\n\n## Citation\n\nCopy\n\n@misc{li2026combinatoricsasymptoticbehaviordouble, title={Combinatorics and asymptotic behavior for double Hurwitz numbers}, author={Xiang Li}, year={2026}, eprint={2604.26323}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math-ph}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.26323}, }--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\namphora/ArXivOpenProblems · Datasets at Hugging Face (https://huggingface.co/datasets/amphora/ArXivOpenProblems/viewer/default/train?p=2939)\nciteturn4search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 6 days ago; 2606.01817#2  | 2606.01817  | https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.01817  | On the product of cross-intersecting families with maximal covering number  | math.CO  | conjecture  | Together with (1.8) for large values of $k$ it seems natural to make the following conjecture. ... 2604.24667#10  | 2604.24667  | https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.24667  | Principal Matroid Determinants  | math.AG  | natural_extension  | It would be interesting to develop a similar strategy for the matroid hypergeometric system . ... 2603.26979#0  | 2603.26979  | https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.26979  | Characterization of the reproducing structure of the Bessel potential spaces beyond p = 2  | math.FA  | open_problem  | Future work Theorem 1 shows that the relation between kernel and spaces is non-unique: given pairs of Bessel potential spaces can be equipped with multiple kernels by choosing a different pairing, and every kernel can serve as kernel for multiple pairs of Bessel potential spaces. ... 2603.17978#1  | 2603.17978  | https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.17978  | On rank 2 hypergeometric motives  | math.NT  | natural_extension  | We mostly restricted to rank two hypergeometric motives